\label{WAC1}
There exists an uncountable set of pairwise non--isomorphic
$2$--generated verbally complete groups.

\label{E(g)}
Let $G$ be a group hyperbolic relative to a collection of
subgroups $\{ H_\lambda \} _{ \lambda \in \Lambda  } $, $g$ a hyperbolic element of infinite order of
$G$. Then the following conditions hold.
\begin{enumerate}
\item The element $g$ is contained in a unique maximal elementary
subgroup $E_G(g)$ of $G$, where $$E_G(g)=\{ f\in G\; :\;
f^{-1}g^nf=g^{\pm n}\; {\rm for \; some\; } n\in \mathbb N\} .$$

\item The group $G$ is hyperbolic relative to the collection
$\{ H_\lambda \} _{ \lambda \in \Lambda  }\cup \{ E_G(g)\} $.
\end{enumerate}

\label{HNN0}
Suppose that a group $G$ is hyperbolic relative to a collection of
subgroups $\{ H_\lambda \} _{ \lambda \in \Lambda  } \cup \{ K\} $ and for some $\nu \in \Lambda $,
there exists a monomorphism $\iota \colon K\to H_{\nu }$. Then the
HNN--extension
\begin{equation}\label{HNN-pres}
\widetilde G=\langle G,t\; |\; t^{-1}kt=\iota (k),\; k\in K\rangle
\end{equation}
is hyperbolic relative to $\{ H_\lambda \} _{ \lambda \in \Lambda  } $.

\label{subseg}
Let $Q$ be a geodesic quadrangle with sides $a,b,c,d$ in a $\delta $-hyperbolic space. Suppose that $l(a)\ge 4\max \{ l(b),\, l(d)\} $. Then
there exist subsegments $p$ , $q$ of the sides $a$ and $b$, respectively, such that $\min \{ l(p), \, l(q)\} \ge \frac{7}{20} l(a) -8\delta $ and ${\rm dist\,} (p_{\pm }, q_\pm )\le 8\delta $.

\label{k-loc}
Let $r$ be a $k$--local geodesic in a $\delta $--hyperbolic metric
space for some $k>8\delta $. Then $r$ is $(1/3, 2\delta
)$--quasi--geodesic.

\label{O52}
Suppose that $\mathcal R$ satisfies the $C(\varepsilon ,\mu, \lambda, c,\rho )$-condition for some values of the parameters. Let $\Delta $ be a reduced diagram over (\ref{quot}). Then for every $\varepsilon $-contiguity subdiagram $\Gamma $ of a cell $\Pi $ to another cell $\Sigma $, we have
\begin{equation}\label{geomSC}
\max \{ (\Pi , \Gamma , \Sigma ),\, (\Sigma , \Gamma , \Pi )\} <\mu .
\end{equation}

Let us fix $\lambda , c>0$.
Observe that the $C_1 (\varepsilon , \mu,\lambda , c, \rho )$--condition becomes stronger as $\lambda $ increases and $c$ decreases. Hence it suffices to prove the lemma assuming that $\lambda < \alpha $ and $c>a$ for $\alpha $ and $a$ provided by Lemma \ref{Un}. Let us choose constants $1/16>\mu >0$, $\varepsilon  >0$, $\rho >0$ such that
\begin{enumerate}
\item[($\ast$)] the conclusion of Lemma \ref{Gr0} holds.
\end{enumerate}
Again as in the proof of Lemma \ref{G1}, we note that the $C_1 (\varepsilon , \mu,\lambda , c, \rho )$--condition becomes stronger as $\mu $ decreases and $\varepsilon , \rho $ increase. Hence we may decrease $\mu$ and then increase $\rho $ and $\varepsilon $ if necessary without violating  ($\ast $). In particular, without loss of generality, we may  assume that
\begin{equation}\label{eps}
\varepsilon  > 2\kappa + 8\delta ,
\end{equation}
where $\kappa =\kappa (\lambda, c)$ is the constant provided by Lemma \ref{qg} and $\delta $ is the hyperbolicity constant of $\Gamma (G, X\cup \mathcal H) $. We fix $\varepsilon  $ from now on.


Suppose that $g$ is an element of order $n>0$ in $G_1$ such that its preimage has infinite order in $G$. Assume also that $g$ has the smallest length among all elements from the conjugacy class $g^{G_1}$. Denote by $U$ a shortest word in the alphabet $X\cup \mathcal H$ representing $g$ in $G_1$. Then there exists a diagram $\Delta $ over (\ref{quot}) with boundary label $U^n$. By Lemma \ref{Un}, the label of $\partial \Delta $ is $(\lambda , c)$--quasi-geodesic (in $G$) and $\Delta $ contains at least one $\mathcal R$-cell. (For otherwise $\Delta $ is a diagram over (\ref{ZP}) and $U^n=1$ in $G$.) Note that passing from $\Delta $ to an $\mathcal O$-equivalent diagram does not affect $\partial \Delta $ and the number of $\mathcal R$-cells. Thus by Lemma \ref{Gr0} we can assume that there exists an $\mathcal R$--cell $\Pi $ in $\Delta $ and $\varepsilon  $--contiguity subdiagram
$\Gamma $ of $\Pi $ to $\partial \Delta $ such that $(\Pi , \Gamma , \partial \Delta )> 1-13\mu .$

Let $\partial \Gamma =s_1q_1s_2q_2$ as on Fig. 1. Since $\Gamma $ has no $\mathcal R$-cells, we may think of $s_1q_1s_2q_2$ as a qadrangle in $\Gamma (G, X\cup \mathcal H) $, where $q_i$ is $(\lambda , c)$-quasi-geodesic, and $l(s_i)\le \varepsilon  $ for $i=1,2$. Therefore we have
\begin{equation}\label{lq2}
\begin{array}{rl}
l(q_2)&\ge dist_{X\cup\mathcal H} ((q_2)_-, (q_2)_+) \ge dist_{X\cup\mathcal H} ((q_1)_-, (q_1)_+)-l(s_1)-l(s_2) \\ &\\ &\ge \lambda l(q_1) -c -2\varepsilon  \ge \lambda (1-13\mu) l(\partial \Pi ) -c-2\varepsilon  \ge \lambda (1-13\mu) \rho -c-2\varepsilon  .
\end{array}
\end{equation}
(Recall again that $l(\partial \Pi)\ge \rho$ by the $C_1 (\varepsilon , \mu,\lambda , c, \rho )$--condition.) In particular, choosing  $\rho $ large enough we can make  $l(q_2)$ as large as we want.


Passing from $U$ to a cyclic shift of $U^{\pm 1}$ if necessary, we may assume that ${\rm\bf Lab\, } (q_2)$ is a prefix of $U^n$ . We now have three cases to consider. Our goal is to show that neither of them is possible whenever $\mu $ is small enough and $\rho $ is large enough.

{\it Case 1.} Suppose that $l(q_2)\ge 4\| U\| /3$. This allows us to find two long disjoint equal subwords of ${\rm\bf Lab\, } (q_2)$. More precisely, we decompose  ${\rm\bf Lab\, } (q_2)$  as ${\rm\bf Lab\, } (q_2)\equiv WV_1WV_2$, where $$\lambda ^2 l(q_2)/5\le \| W\| \le \lambda ^2l(q_2)/4$$ and $$\| V_1\| >  l(q_2)/3.$$  Let $q_2=w_1v_1w_2v_2$ be the corresponding decomposition of the path $q_2$. Corollary \ref{qgq} applied to the quadrangle $s_1q_1s_2q_2$ implies that there is a point $o\in q_1$ such that $dist_{X\cup\mathcal H} (o, (w_1)_+)\le K +\varepsilon $, where $K$ depends on $\lambda$, $c$, and $\delta $ only. Let $q_1^{-1}=r_1t$, where $(r_1)_+=o$. Thus we have
\begin{equation}\label{rw1}
dist_{X\cup\mathcal H} ((r_1)_\pm , (w_1)_\pm )\le K+\varepsilon 
\end{equation}
Similarly one can find a subpath $r_2^\xi $, $\xi =\pm 1$, of $q_1^{-1}$ such that
\begin{equation}\label{rw2}
dist_{X\cup\mathcal H} ((r_2^\xi)_\pm , (w_2)_\pm )\le K+\varepsilon  .
\end{equation}

We note that $r_1$ and $r_2$ are disjoint. Indeed otherwise $r_1$ passes through $(r_2)_-$ or $(r_2)_+$. For definiteness, assume that $(r_2)_-\in r_1$. Then we have
$$
\begin{array}{rl}
l(r_1) & \le \frac1\lambda (dist_{X\cup\mathcal H} ((r_1)_-, (r_1)_+) +c)\le \frac1\lambda (l(w_1) +2\varepsilon  + K+c)\\ &\\ & = \frac1\lambda (\|W\| +2\varepsilon  + K+c)\le
\lambda  l(q_2)/4 + (2\varepsilon  +K+c)/\lambda .
\end{array}
$$
On the other hand,
$$
\begin{array}{rl}
l(r_1)& \ge dist_{X\cup\mathcal H} ((r_1)_-, (r_2)_-) \ge \lambda l(w_1v_1) -c-2\varepsilon  -K\ge  \\ &\\ & \lambda \| V_1\| -c-2\varepsilon  -K \ge \lambda l(q_2)/3 -c-2\varepsilon  -K.
\end{array}
$$
These inequalities contradict each other if $l(q_2)$ is large enough. As explained before, the later condition can always be ensured by choosing sufficiently large $\rho $ (see (\ref{lq2})).

Thus we have a decomposition $q_1^{-1} =r_1t_1r_2^{\xi }t_2$, $\xi =\pm 1$. Let ${\rm\bf Lab\, } (q_1)^{-1} \equiv R_1T_1R_2T_2$ be the corresponding decomposition of the label.  By (\ref{rw1}) and (\ref{rw2}), we have $R_1=Y_1WZ_1$ and $R_2= Y_2W^{\pm 1} Z_2$ in $G$, where $\| Y_i\|, \| X_i\| \le K+\varepsilon  $ for $i=1,2$. Hence $R_1=YR_2^{\pm 1 } Z$ in $G$, where  $\| Y\| , \| Z\| \le 2(K +\varepsilon )$. Without loss of generality, we may assume that words $Y$ and $Z$ are geodesic in $G$. Let $aybz$ be the corresponding rectangle in $\Gamma (G, X\cup \mathcal H)$, where ${\rm\bf Lab\, } (a)\equiv R_1^{-1}$, ${\rm\bf Lab\, } (y)\equiv Y$, ${\rm\bf Lab\, } (b)\equiv R_2^{\pm 1}$, ${\rm\bf Lab\, } (z)\equiv Z$.  Recall that $R_1, R_2$ are subwords of the $(\lambda , c)$--quasi-geodesic word ${\rm\bf Lab\, } (\partial \Pi )$. Hence $a,b$ are $(\lambda , c)$--quasi-geodesic. Using (\ref{lq2}) and the triangle inequality, we obtain
\begin{equation}\label{lala}
\begin{array}{rl}
l (a)& =\| R_1\| \ge dist_{X\cup\mathcal H} ((r_1)_-, (r_1)_-) \\ & \\ &\ge  dist_{X\cup\mathcal H} ((w_1)_-, (w_1)_+) -l(s_1) -dist_{X\cup\mathcal H} ((r_1)_+, (w_1)_+)\\ & \\ &  \ge \lambda l(w_1) -c-2\varepsilon  -K= \lambda \| W\| -c - 2\varepsilon  - K\\ & \\ &  \ge \lambda ^3 l(q_2) /5 -c-2\varepsilon  -K \\ & \\ & \ge \lambda ^3[\lambda (1-13\mu) \rho -c-2\varepsilon  ]/5 -c-2\varepsilon  -K
\end{array}
\end{equation}
Clearly we can ensure $l(a)>(4\max\{ l(y), l(z)\} +c)/\lambda $ by taking large enough $\rho$. This allows us to apply Corollary \ref{subsegcor}. Thus we obtain subsegments $a^\prime , b^\prime $ of the sides $a$ and $b$, respectively, such that the distances between the corresponding ends of these subsegments is at most $8\delta +2\kappa $. Let $A={\rm\bf Lab\, } (a^\prime )$, $B={\rm\bf Lab\, } (b^\prime )$. Then $A=CB^{\pm 1}D$ in $G$, where $\| C\| , \| D\| \le 8\delta +2\kappa <\varepsilon $ by (\ref{eps}). Using this and (\ref{lala}), we obtain
$$
\min \{ \| A\|, \| B\| \}  \ge \frac{7}{20} (\lambda l(a) -c) -8\delta - 2\kappa \ge \mu\rho \ge \mu l(\partial \Pi )
$$
if $\mu $ is small enough and $\rho $ is large enough. Since $A$ and $B$ are disjoint subwords of ${\rm\bf Lab\, } (\partial \Pi )$, this contradicts the $C_1 (\varepsilon , \mu,\lambda , c, \rho )$--condition.


{\bf Case 2.} Suppose that $\| U\| \le l(q_2)\le 4\| U\| /3$, i.e. ${\rm\bf Lab\, } (q_2)\equiv UV$ for some (may be empty) word $V$, $\|V\|\le \| U\| /3$.
Note that ${\rm\bf Lab\, } (q_2)={\rm\bf Lab\, }(s_2^{-1}us_1^{-1})$
in $G_1$, hence $g={\rm\bf Lab\, }(s_2^{-1}us_1^{-1})V^{-1}$ in $G_1$. Since $U$ is the shortest word representing $g$ in $G_1$, we obtain
$$
\| U\| \le 2\varepsilon  +13\mu l(\partial \Pi)  +\| V\| \le 2\varepsilon  +13\mu l(\partial \Pi) + \| U\|/3.
$$
Consequently,
\begin{equation}\label{lule}
\| U \| \le 3(2\varepsilon  +13\mu l(\partial \Pi))/2\le 3(2\varepsilon  +13\mu \rho )/2.
\end{equation}
On the other hand, using (\ref{lq2}) we obtain
$$
\| U\| \ge 3l(q_2)/4 \ge 3(\lambda (1-13\mu) \rho -c-2\varepsilon  )/4.
$$
The later inequality contradicts (\ref{lule}) whenever $\mu $ is small enough and $\rho $ is large enough.

{\bf Case 3.} Suppose that $ l(q_2)< \| U\| $, i.e., ${\rm\bf Lab\, } (q_2) $ is a subword of $U$. Again since $U$ is the shortest word representing $g$ in $G_1$, we have $\| {\rm\bf Lab\, } (q_2)\| \le \| Q\| $ for every word $Q$ such that $Q={\rm\bf Lab\, } (q_2)$ in $G_1$. In particular, for $Q\equiv {\rm\bf Lab\, } (s_2^{-1}us_1^{-1})$, we obtain
\begin{equation}\label{Qge}
\| Q\| \ge  \| {\rm\bf Lab\, } (q_2)\| =l(q_2) \ge \lambda (1-13\mu) \rho -c-2\varepsilon  .
\end{equation}
by (\ref{lq2}). On the other hand, we obviously have $\| Q\| \le 2\varepsilon  +13\mu  l(\partial \Pi )<2\varepsilon  +13\mu\rho$, which contradicts (\ref{Qge}) if $\mu $ is small enough and $\rho $ is large enough.

\label{quad}
Suppose that $upv^{-1}q^{-1}$ is an an arbitrary quadrangle in $\Gamma (G, X\cup \mathcal H)
$ satisfying the following conditions:

(a) ${\rm\bf Lab\, } (p)\equiv {\rm\bf Lab\, } (q^{-1})\in \mathcal {SW} $;

(b) $\max\{ l(u), \, l(v)\} \le \varepsilon  $;

(c) $l(p)=l(q)\ge 6\varepsilon  +22$.

\noindent Then the paths $p$ and $q$ have a common edge.

\label{me1} Let us divide $p$ into three parts $p=p_1p_0p_2$ such that
\begin{equation}\label{p1p2}
l(p_1)=l(p_2)=3\varepsilon  +6.
\end{equation}
Suppose that $s$ is a component of $p_0$. Then $s$ can not be
connected to a component of paths $u$ or $v$.

\label{f1f2}
Suppose that for some
$\lambda , \mu \in \Lambda $, $\lambda \ne \mu $, $H_\lambda $ and $H_\mu $ contain elements of infinite order and $H_\lambda \cap H_\mu=\{ 1\} $. Then there are $f\in H_\lambda $, $g\in H_\mu $ such that $fg$ is a hyperbolic element of infinite order and $E_G(fg)=\langle fg \rangle $.

Let $S_i$  denote the sum of lengths of all arcs of type (A1) in $\partial \Delta _i$, $i=1, \ldots , d$. Assume that $S\ge n\sqrt{\rho }$. Then
\begin{equation}\label{621}
S_i\ge n_i\sqrt{\rho}/60
\end{equation}
 for some $i$. Indeed otherwise we have
$$
S=\sum\limits_{i=1}^d S_i\le\frac{\sqrt{\rho}}{60} \sum\limits_{i=1}^d n_i
< n\sqrt{\rho}
$$
By Lemma \ref{61}.

For every $i=1, \ldots , d$, the diagram $\Delta _i$ has at most $n_i$ holes. Hence we can cut it by $l\le n_i$ paths $t_1, \ldots, t_l$ into a simply connected diagram $\widetilde{\Delta _i}$. We assume that the collection of cutting paths $t_1, \ldots , t_l$ is chosen so that the sum $\sum\limits_{i=1}^l l(t_i) $ is minimal. The boundary of $\widetilde{\Delta _i}$ decomposes into $k_i$ subpaths, each of which is either $t_j^{\pm 1}$ for some $j=1, \ldots , l$, or an arc of one of the types (A1)-(A3) (or a part of such an arc arising after cutting along $t_1, \ldots , t_l$). Therefore,
\begin{equation}\label{622}
k_i\le 4n_i.
\end{equation}


Note also that passing to a diagram which is $\mathcal O$-equivalent to $\Delta $ if necessary, we may assume that the labels of $t_i$'s are geodesic in $G$ without loss of generality. Indeed if $w_i$ is a geodesic word representing the same element as ${\rm\bf Lab\, } (t_i)$ in $G$, let $\Sigma _i$ be a diagram over (\ref{ZP}) with boundary label $w_i({\rm\bf Lab\, } (t_i))^{-1}$. Further let $\Xi _i$ be the diagram obtained by gluing $\Sigma _i$ and its mirror copy along $w_i$. Clearly ${\rm\bf Lab\, } \partial (\Xi_i)={\rm\bf Lab\, } (t_i)({\rm\bf Lab\, } (t_i))^{-1}$. We can use $0$-refinement (see the subsection ``Van Kampen diagrams" in Section 3) to create a copy $t_i^\prime $ of the paths $t_i$ in $\Delta $ with the same label and endpoints such that $t_i(t_i^\prime )^{-1}$ bounds a subdiagram consisting of $0$-cells. Further we cut this subdiagram and fill in the obtained hole with $\Xi_i$. After this transformation, the vertices $(t_i)_-$ and $(t_i)_+$ are connected by a path whose label $w_i$ is geodesic in $G$, and we can replace $t_i$ with that paths. Note that this transformation does not affect $\partial \Delta $, $\mathcal R$-cells, and distinguished $\varepsilon $-contiguity subdiagrams.

Similarly we may assume that for every (sub)arc $p$ of an arc of type (A1)-(A3) in $\partial \widetilde{\Delta _i}$, the diagram $\widetilde{\Delta _i}$ contains a path $\bar p$ with the same endpoints as $p$ such that ${\rm\bf Lab\, } (\bar p)$ is geodesic in $G$ and $p(\bar p)^{-1}$ bounds a subdiagram over (\ref{ZP}). Since $\widetilde{\Delta _i}$ is a simply connected diagram over (\ref{ZP}), its $1$-skeleton can be naturally mapped to $\Gamma (G, \mathcal A)$ by a map preserving labels and orientation. In what follows we keep the same notation $t_i^{\pm 1}$, $p$, etc., for the images of the paths $t_i^{\pm 1}$, $p$, etc., in $\Gamma (G, \mathcal A) $ (although $t_i^{-1}$ is not the inverse path of $t_i$ there). In particular, $p$ and $\bar p$ belong to the closed $\kappa $-neighborhoods of each other in $\Gamma (G, \mathcal A)$ by Lemma \ref{qg}.

In the notation of Lemma \ref{N123}, let $N_2$ consist of images of segments $t_i^{\pm 1}$, $i=1, \ldots , l$, in $\Gamma (G, \mathcal A) $ and images of $\bar p$ for (sub)arcs $p$ of type (A2). Further let $N_1$ and $N_3$ consist of images of $\bar p$ for (sub)arcs $p$ of type (A1) and (A3), respectively.

Clearly $\sigma _3\le \varepsilon  k_i$. On the other hand, by (\ref{621}) and (\ref{622}) we obtain
$$
\begin{array}{rl}
\sigma _1 & = \sum \limits_{\bar p\in N_1} l(p_1) \ge \sum \limits_{\bar p\in N_1} (\lambda l(p) - c)\ge \lambda S_i-k_ic> \lambda n_i \sqrt{\rho}/60 -k_ic \\ & \\ & \ge
\lambda k_i\sqrt{\rho} /240 -k_i c =k_i(\lambda \sqrt{\rho } /240 - c).
\end{array}
$$
Taking large enough $\rho $, we may assume that $a=\lambda \sqrt{\rho } /240 - c>\max\{ 1000\varepsilon  , c_2\} $. Hence by Lemma \ref{N123}, there exist subsegments $\bar q_j$, $j=1,2$, of  some $\bar p_1\in N_1$ and $\bar p_2\in N_1\cup N_2$, respectively, such that $l(p_j)\ge 10^{-3} a$, $j=1,2$, and the distance between the respective endpoints of $p_1$ and $p_2$ is at most $c_1$. This means that there exist
subsegments $q_j$ of $p_j$, $j=1,2$, of lengths at least  $10^{-3}a -2\kappa$ such that
\begin{equation}\label{q1q2dist}
\max\{ dist_{X\cup\mathcal H} ((q_1)_-, (q_2)_-),\, dist_{X\cup\mathcal H} ((q_1)_+, (q_2)_+)\} \le 2\kappa +c_1<\varepsilon .
\end{equation}
The later inequality uses (\ref{eck}). Now there are two cases to consider.

{\it Case 1.} $\bar p_2\in N_1$. That is, $q_1$ and $q_2$ are subpaths of boundaries of some $\mathcal R$-cells in $\Delta $. After passing to an $\mathcal O$-equivalent diagram if necessary, we may assume that there exists a subdiagram $\Gamma $ of $\Delta $ such that $\partial \Gamma =q_1s_1q_2^{-1}s_2$, where $l(s_j)<\varepsilon $, $j=1,2$, and $\Gamma $ does not intersect $\mathcal R$-cells and distinguished $\varepsilon  $-contiguity subdiagrams. This contradicts the maximality of $\mathcal M$.

\begin{figure}
   \hspace{27mm} \includegraphics{cut.eps}\\
   \vspace{-3mm}
  \caption{Replacing a cutting path with a shorter one.}\label{cut}
\end{figure}


{\it Case 2.} $\bar p_2\in N_2$. If $q_2$ is a subpaths of $\partial \Delta $, we get a contradiction as above by constructing a new $\varepsilon  $-contiguity subdiagram of an $\mathcal R$--cell to $\partial \Delta $. Thus we only need to deal with the case $p_2=t_j^{\pm 1}$. Without loss of generality, we may assume that $p_2=t_j$. Let $t_j=uq_2v$. By (\ref{q1q2dist}), after passing to an $\mathcal O$-equivalent diagram if necessary, we can find a paths $s$ of lengths at most $\varepsilon $ in $\Delta $ connecting $(q_2)_-$ to $(q_1)_-$ (see Fig. \ref{cut}). Note that
$$l(us)\le l(t_j)- l(q_2) +\varepsilon  \le l(t_j) - (10^{-3}(\lambda \sqrt{\rho } /240 - c) -2\kappa)  +\varepsilon  <l(t_j)$$ if $\rho $ is large enough. This contradicts our assumption that
$\sum\limits_{i=1}^l l(t_i) $ is minimal.

Thus in both cases we obtain a contradiction. Hence $S< n\sqrt{\rho }$.

\label{63}
Suppose that $\Delta $ satisfies ($\ast $). Let $\Sigma _0$ and $\Sigma $ denote the sum  of lengths of all outer arcs and the sum of perimeters of all $\mathcal R$-cells in $\Delta $, respectively. Then
$\Sigma _0 > (1-11\mu )\Sigma $.
